WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.000
Hi, well, welcome to Lecture 25, the General Linear Model.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.000
So, so far in this course, we've learned a lot about models and how the parameters work and how things are related.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
So, today we're going to go a little bit further and we're trying to learn more about the...

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
So, we've looked at numerical covariance, we've looked at ANOVA type factors.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And so, now we're actually like, what can we do? We can stick them all in one model.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:43.000
We call these a General Linear Model, which, unfortunately, there's also something called a Generalized Linear Model,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
which are going to be covered, we'll cover those a bit later. So, these are two different things.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
But the main question we're trying to address with these models is, if we add a factor to regression,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.000
does it change the intercept, the slope, or both? And so, we're going to go through an example today.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
First, we're going to give you the mathematical underpinnings, how this is going to work.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.000
And then we'll go through an example together to show you how it's working.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
So, if you remember, when we fit factors inside of linear models, or essentially just indicator variables,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
we've got this so-called design matrix that we use, that if you were to actually look at what's happening

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
under the hood when we're fitting these models, it's just a 0 or a 1. So, does this factor exist? Yes.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Does this factor exist? Yes. And there's a couple ways you could imagine you could do that.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
You can do everything versus a given level. So, say you have three levels, small, medium, and large.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:03.000
You could say, my baseline is small, and then medium is the difference from small to medium,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:12.000
and large is the difference from small to large. Or, you could say, I want to fit small, medium, and large,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
and they're not differences from each other. So, you'd end up with the same estimate, it's just the way you parameterize it.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:28.000
By default, in most software packages I've used, we just pick something as the reference level.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
So, you'll say that, you know, Maori's the reference level, or New Zealand European's the reference level,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
and everything's compared to that, or has a home as the reference.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
And so, typically you'll choose the reference to either be the most common, but in software packages,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:51.000
you'll often choose the lowest alphabet number. Alphabet number, sorry, the alphabet letter.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:59.000
And so, let's see, what else do we have here? And then we have something where,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:06.000
and if we start adding interactions between factors, numerical covariates, we can allow them to have different slopes.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:14.000
So, let's take a look at this a bit more. Let's look at the images first.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
So, these are the models you've been making. This could potentially be adding another factor that gives you another level.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
And this is saying, hey, I've got two separate, not only do I have different groups, but I have different slopes in them.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:37.000
So, more formally, if we have response variable y, a numerical covariate x, this is your standard linear regression,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
and now we're adding in a factor, we'll call that a, we can make the models by saying what the factor's allowed to change.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
If the factor doesn't do anything, we can just do this.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
We can essentially just stick in, and we can adjust for a factor, and it doesn't actually change.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
And we get one slope, and we get one intercept, and that's all it does.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:08.000
But if the factor allows the line to shift up and down, but the slope remains consistent, it'd be something like this.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.000
So, now factor group one to factor group two, the slope is the same.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:21.000
So, we're not fitting, we're still fitting a unified slope, but we're allowing the factor to say kind of where the intercept is.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
This holds a lot of times.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Kind of, if you think of maybe heights of people growing, some people might start off higher or lower,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.000
but they're going to take roughly the same trajectory.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:46.000
And this is a much more complex relationship here where we can say, does the relationship with x depend on the level of a?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
So, this is actually an interaction. So, the slopes are going to differ, and so we're going to say,

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
whatever group this is, is growing slowly, and this one, whoa, shooting up like a rocket.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.000
And so, we're going to spend most of the time on these two here in this lecture.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Trying to see what questions we can answer with this, and what we can do with it.

